  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe area of low pressure that has been giving us clouds and showers \nwill begin to move out of the region, bringing clearer and drier \nweather today. Behind it, northerly flow aloft will allow for weaker \nSanta Ana winds to funnel through the mountains and valleys of San \nBernardino and Riverside Counties this morning and afternoon. Most \nareas will be 5-10 degrees warmer than yesterday. These winds die \noff by Sunday, as a weak troughing pattern prevails over the region \ninto the middle of next week. High temperatures creep up a bit more \non Sunday with a better chance to see highs reach 80 degrees across \nthe western valleys. Little day to day changes can be expected \nSunday through about Wednesday with mostly clear skies and minimal \nchanges in temperatures with highs within 5 degrees of average. \n\nThe weather across SoCal becomes more interesting for the latter \nhalf of next week as a troughing pattern develops over the West \nCoast. An area of low pressure is expected to develop around \nWednesday across the Pacific Northwest. Models show larger scale \ntroughing starting to develop over the West Coast by Thursday into \nnext weekend, but to what magnitude and location is still yet to be \ndetermined. Confidence is starting to increase on seeing \ntemperatures cool back to below average during this period. Ensemble \nmodel guidance begins to show increasing chances for precipitation \naround next Friday into the weekend. NBM rain chances match with \nthis general idea for the official forecast. Since we are one week \nout, timing and exact precipitation amounts are greatly uncertain. \nPlease be prepared for more wet weather impacts, especially as you \nstart to make those Halloween weekend plans.",
